-- allium: 3
-- db.allium
--
-- The processing model of a single database within an XTDB node:
-- transaction submission → log processing → block flushing → query.
-- Compaction job selection and algorithm are specified in compaction.allium;
-- the compaction message flow is here.
--
-- Scope: one database (e.g. "xtdb" or a secondary).
-- External: the multi-database orchestration layer (attach/detach).
--
-- Source and replica are always separate logs (invariant, not just convention).
-- A LogProcessor owns the indexing pipeline lifecycle per database.
-- Compactor is a peer at db-system level, not a child of the processor.
-- Leader/follower transitions driven by the processor's own reading of the replica log.
-- A single LiveIndex sits outside the processor as shared state.
-- Leader mode feeds it from its own replica-log CONSUME-BACK; follower mode feeds it
-- from replica processing.
-- A single Watchers instance tracks both txId and sourceMsgId for client correlation,
-- plus the latest ExternalSourceToken for external-source databases.
-- The compactor writes L1+ to the source log; the leader forwards to replica.
--
-- Leader pipelining — resolve / append / consume-back / apply.
--   The leader applies its OWN writes only once it has read them BACK off its own
--   replica log at its own TERM (Raft's ReadIndex): that consume-back IS the fence.
--   The term IS the RESOLVER: a select loop over the source log, external-source txs and
--   GC tasks, plus an APPLY arm servicing the consume-back channel, so a tx cannot
--   interleave a partial state. It starts three children:
--     - APPEND PUMP: serialise, then a plain term-stamped append.
--     - GC.
--     - SOURCE-log tail.
--   Reading the REPLICA log is not the term's. One reader per partition does it, and
--   hands each record to whichever role is live. It outlives every term, so a term
--   ending surrenders no position.
--   Mechanism, ownership and the durability-handle failure legs: live-index.allium.
--   Role lifecycle and the term: log-processor-lifecycle.allium.
--
-- Two heads, two homes — callers choose:
--   RESOLVED head (TxResolver.latest_completed_tx): the highest tx resolved, and so
--     staged. Drives resolution — next external-source tx_id, system-time smoothing,
--     read-your-writes.
--   DURABLE head (LiveIndex.latest_completed_tx): the highest tx applied, i.e. consumed
--     back and imported. The QUERY BASIS. Resolved leads; durable lags by everything
--     staged but not yet applied.
--
-- Two source watermarks, and the tell that they differ:
--   The leader's RESOLVE-side watermark (Leader.last_resolved_src_msg_id) is what gets
--   STAMPED onto each replica record. The DURABLE one lives on Watchers and advances only
--   on apply. Stamping from the watchers instead would regress notifyTx's monotonicity
--   check, because a source tx resolved behind an external-source tx applies first and
--   pushes the watermark past the stale stamp (#5817).
--
-- Followers and the transition processor have no resolver and no consume-back: every
-- message they import is already durable, so they import straight into the durable live
-- index and advance the watchers as they go.
--
-- A query MUST NEVER see a tx the leader has not read back: external snapshots open at
-- the durable LiveIndex.latest_completed_tx.
--
-- One leader variant, not two. An external source is an OPTIONAL COLLABORATOR of the
-- leader rather than a separate processor: flush / tries / block coordination and the
-- whole resolve → append → consume-back → apply pipeline are identical either way, and
-- the two differ only in where a tx enters and how its tx_id is assigned.
--   - No external source: client-driven. Subscribes to the source log, resolves
--     TxMessages, and takes each tx_id from the source-log position.
--   - External source configured: transactions arrive via the TxIndexer callback, and
--     tx_id is assigned off the RESOLVED head. Client submitTx is rejected with
--     xtdb/submit-tx-to-external-source-db. The source log is still consumed, for
--     flush / tries / block coordination only.

use "./trie-cat.allium" as trie_cat
use "./compaction.allium" as compaction

---- Given

given {
    database: Database
    log_processor: LogProcessor
    tx_resolver: TxResolver?           -- leader-only (persister-owned); absent in follower mode
    live_index: LiveIndex              -- shared across leader/follower modes
    watchers: Watchers                 -- single instance: correlates source msg_ids and tx_ids with client futures
    table_catalog: TableCatalog
    block_uploader: BlockUploader      -- shared by leader, transition processor
    compactor: Compactor.ForDatabase   -- peer of log_processor at db-system level
    storage_format: StorageFormat      -- this node's current storage/buffer_pool format
}

---- External Entities

external entity Client {
    -- The party submitting transactions.
}

external entity Throwable {
    -- A JVM exception. Opaque here: the abort cause on a resolved transaction or a
    -- caller-fault dbOp, surfaced to the client but never inspected by this spec's rules.
}

external entity Log {
    -- An ordered, append-only message stream.
    -- Two separate instances per database: source_log and replica_log.
    -- They MUST be distinct logs (not aliases for the same underlying log).
    -- See: api/log/Log.kt
}

external entity ObjectStore {
    -- Durable key-value storage for blocks, tries, table-blocks.
}

external entity ExternalSource {
    -- A system outside XTDB that feeds a database via CDC
    -- (e.g. Postgres logical replication). On assignment the external source
    -- calls back through a TxIndexer, providing an opaque resume token and
    -- a writer that populates an OpenTx with the transformed row data.
    -- An external-source database assigns its own monotonically increasing
    -- tx_id off the RESOLVED head (tx_resolver.latest_completed_tx.tx_id + 1)
    -- rather than taking it from the source log position. Client submitTx is
    -- rejected for these databases — transactions enter only through the
    -- external source.
    -- See: api/tx/ExternalSource.kt
}

---- Entities

entity LiveIndex {
    -- See: indexer/LiveIndex.kt, live-index.allium
    -- Single instance shared across leader and follower modes.
    --
    -- latest_completed_tx — DURABLE head / QUERY BASIS: the highest tx in the durable
    --   live tables. External snapshots open at this. On a leader it is advanced by
    --   apply on CONSUME-BACK (so it lags the resolved head by everything staged but
    --   not yet read back); on a follower by importTx, which has no lag
    --   because every message it imports is already durable.
    -- The RESOLVED head is NOT a field here: on a leader it lives on the resolver (see
    --   TxResolver). LiveIndex is resolver-agnostic — it knows only the durable layer.
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey?
    is_full: Boolean

    -- Rows already applied into the currently-open block. Seeds the leader's
    -- rows_since_block gauge, so a leader inheriting a partially-filled block from
    -- replay cuts it where the previous leader would have.
    block_row_count: Integer

    -- Derived
    has_data: latest_completed_tx != null
}

entity Database {
    -- See: database/Database.kt
    source_log: Log
    replica_log: Log

    -- Configured external source, if any.
    -- This — not the local processor's mode — is what rejects a client submitTx: the source log is shared, so any node's submit path has to make the same decision whether or not it happens to be the leader.
    -- Opened and closed with the database, on each node that could lead it, so one instance spans every leader term that node serves.
    -- A node that indexes nothing, or holds the database read-only, can never lead and opens no source.
    external_source: ExternalSource?

    invariant DistinctLogs {
        source_log != replica_log
    }
}

entity LogProcessor {
    -- See: indexer/LogProcessor.kt
    -- Owns the indexing pipeline lifecycle for a database.
    -- Starts in follower mode. Always in exactly one mode.
    --
    -- latest_replica_msg_id: the replica-log position this processor has reached — on a
    --   leader, the CONSUME-BACK position (how far it has read its own writes back); on
    --   a follower, its tail position. It seeds the next mode on every handover, which
    --   is why every mode tracks it.
    --
    -- The SOURCE-log watermark is deliberately NOT here. The durable one lives on
    --   Watchers (advanced on apply, by every mode); the leader additionally keeps a
    --   resolve-side one, ahead of it, on the Leader variant. Two watermarks that lead
    --   and lag each other are two fields, not one.
    --
    -- Transitions are driven by the processor's own replica-log reader:
    --   a claim of its own read back, having conferred -> TransitionToLeader
    --   its leader term ending -> TransitionToFollower
    -- log-processor-lifecycle.allium owns the role state machine; `mode` here is the
    -- coarser view db.allium's rules need.
    mode: Leader | Follower
    latest_replica_msg_id: MessageId

    -- READ-SIDE TERM FENCE: the highest leader term seen so far, seeded from the last
    -- persisted block boundary (table_catalog.boundary_term_id) so it survives restart.
    -- A record BELOW this was written by a superseded leader and is discarded. See the
    -- Fencing section.
    --
    -- Scoped to the partition, not to a mode, and folded before the record reaches either.
    -- Re-seeded at each handover it would forget the terms written since the last flush,
    -- and admit the same term twice.
    max_leader_term: Integer
}

variant Leader : LogProcessor {
    -- The sole writer of this database's replica log for its term. Resolves source-log
    -- messages and external-source txs, appends them (plain, term-stamped), reads them
    -- back and applies them. Also owns block finishing via BlockUploader, and the trie
    -- and block garbage collectors.
    -- See: indexer/LeaderLogProcessor.kt, indexer/LeaderDriver.kt
    --
    -- A promotion's pending block is finished through this processor, before it reads its first source message.
    --   - The boundary the outgoing leader cut is uploaded under the INCOMING term — see BlockUploader.
    --   - The follower's buffered records then replay in log order, a record whose term is below the highest the replay has reached advancing the watermark alone.
    --
    -- external_source is present iff the database has one configured; it makes this
    -- leader external-source-fed rather than client-driven (see the header).
    external_source: ExternalSource?

    -- This leader's fencing term, claimed one above the highest it had read from the replica log (see log-processor-lifecycle.allium).
    -- Stamped onto every replica record this term writes, and checked on consume-back.
    leader_term: Integer

    -- RESOLVE-side source watermark: the source-log position as of the last-resolved tx.
    -- Stamped onto each replica record. Leads watchers.latest_source_msg_id, which only
    -- advances on apply — see the header.
    last_resolved_src_msg_id: MessageId

    -- The last non-null external-source token seen on the resolve side. Carried onto a
    -- BlockBoundary so the persisted Block holds a live resume point.
    last_resolved_ext_token: ExternalSourceToken?

    -- RESOLVE-side block gauge: rows resolved into the currently-open block. The boundary
    -- is cut off this rather than live_index.is_full, which lags (it reflects only APPLIED
    -- txs). Seeded from live_index.block_row_count; reset when a boundary is injected.
    rows_since_block: Integer

    -- A block cut is in progress: the BlockBoundary has been appended but its
    -- BlockUploaded has not been produced. Resolution is PAUSED while true — the
    -- source-log, external-source and GC arms are excluded from the select — so no tx
    -- interleaves between the boundary and its upload, keeping the follower's bounded
    -- pending-block buffer empty. See live-index.allium "Block cut".
    block_in_progress: Boolean

    pending_block: PendingBlock?  -- set between BlockBoundary and BlockUploaded during the upload
}

variant Follower : LogProcessor {
    -- Applies the replica-log records the partition's reader hands it — that reader is the node's sole replica-log consumer, whichever role is live.
    -- Feeds the shared live_index from replica messages, all of which are already durable.
    -- Exposes pending_block state for handoff when a claim of this node's confers.
    -- See: indexer/FollowerLogProcessor.kt
    pending_block: PendingBlock?
}

entity TxResolver {
    -- The leader's transaction resolver, opaque here: its full structure (the FIFO of
    -- resolved-but-not-yet-applied txs and their relations, the read-your-writes staged
    -- segments, the per-tx durability handles and their failure legs) lives in
    -- live-index.allium. db.allium does not `use` live-index.allium, so it names only
    -- what its own rules need.
    -- See: indexer/TxResolver.kt, live-index.allium TxResolver
    --
    -- Standing state the leader owns from construction and frees in its two-phase close
    -- (see TransitionToFollower). NOT a field on LiveIndex — the leader's persister
    -- coroutine is its sole accessor, so it needs no lock. Absent on followers and the
    -- transition processor, which only import already-durable messages.
    --
    -- RESOLVED head: the highest tx resolved, and so staged. Drives resolution — the next
    -- external-source tx_id, system-time smoothing, read-your-writes. Seeded from the
    -- durable head; leads it by exactly the txs staged but not yet applied.
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey?

    -- The queue itself is NOT modelled here — live-index.allium declares it, and derives
    -- its own is_empty from it. What db.allium relies on is the ORDER PROPERTY that makes
    -- the queue head predictable: resolution order = append order = consume-back order,
    -- and terms are unique per leader. So an equal-term ResolvedTx read back is always the
    -- matching head, which is why the apply arm can import without re-materialising. It is
    -- checked rather than assumed (see LeaderAppliesOwnTx).
}

entity TableCatalog {
    -- What one database partition knows about its tables, and the block that knowledge is
    -- current to. The two are one value, replaced wholesale, so a reader sees a block index
    -- against that block's table metadata and never against the previous block's.
    -- See: catalog/TableCatalog.kt, table_catalog.clj
    latest_block: Block?
    table_metadata: Map<trie_cat/TableRef, TableMetadata>

    -- Derived
    current_block_index: latest_block?.block_index
    boundary_replica_msg_id: latest_block?.boundary_replica_msg_id
    -- Seeds a follower's max_leader_term, which is what makes the fence survive restart.
    boundary_term_id: latest_block?.term_id ?? 0
}

entity Watchers {
    -- Single instance per database.
    -- Tracks both txId and sourceMsgId progression, plus the external-source
    -- resume token for external-source databases. These are the DURABLE watermarks:
    -- every mode advances them only once a message has been applied.
    -- Clients call awaitTx(txId) to wait for a transaction result.
    -- A cut-over reads latestSourceMsgId for its source tail's resume point, and waits on nothing: it applied its own claim before judging it.
    --
    -- notifyTx(result, srcMsgId, externalSourceToken): advances txId and
    --   sourceMsgId; if the token is non-null it overrides the tracked token
    --   (so restarts can read the latest resume point from the watcher).
    -- notifyMsg(srcMsgId): advances only sourceMsgId (for non-tx messages like
    --   TriesAdded, BlockBoundary, BlockUploaded, and a NoOp carrying one).
    -- notifyError(exception): transitions to Failed state; all subsequent awaits throw.
    --
    -- See: api/log/Watchers.kt
    latest_tx_id: MessageId
    latest_source_msg_id: MessageId
    latest_tx_result: TransactionResult?
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?

    -- Monotonicity obligations enforced at runtime (temporal, not state properties):
    -- notifyTx must supply txId > latest_tx_id (strictly increasing)
    -- notifyTx and notifyMsg must supply srcMsgId >= latest_source_msg_id (non-decreasing)
    --   >= not >: BlockBoundary can carry the same source msgId as the preceding
    --   ResolvedTx when the block was triggered by the row gauge (no FlushBlock in between).
}

entity BlockUploader {
    -- The leader's, for finishing its own blocks and for completing the one a promotion inherited.
    --
    -- uploadBlock(boundaryMsgId, termId, boundaryMessage):
    --   1. Finishes live index block (liveIndex.finishBlock), yielding L0 tries — one per
    --      table that took rows in this block, and none at all for a table that took none.
    --      A block cut with no transactions in it therefore writes no tries whatsoever
    --   2. Registers those L0 tries in the trie catalog
    --   3. Finishes table catalog, uploads one per-table block file for EVERY table the
    --      table catalog knows — not only the tables from step 1. Block GC's per-table
    --      sweep depends on this (block-gc.allium, EveryTableBlockAtCurrentIndex)
    --   4. Builds and persists the block file — carrying termId, which is what seeds a
    --      restarted follower's fence
    --   5. Refreshes the table catalog, then appends BlockUploaded to the replica log
    --      (a PLAIN, term-stamped append — there is no producer)
    --   6. Calls liveIndex.nextBlock()
    --   7. Off this coroutine, and in this order: posts the L0 tries to the SOURCE log as
    --      TriesAdded, then compactor.signalBlock(). Off-coroutine because uploadBlock runs
    --      on the source log's sole consumer, so appending inline self-deadlocks when the
    --      source-log buffer saturates at a boundary.
    --   Returns the replica msg_id of the BlockUploaded message.
    --
    -- termId is the APPENDING term, which is not always the boundary's: a transition
    -- finishes the previous leader's pending block, and its BlockUploaded must carry the
    -- NEW term or followers that have already advanced would fence it and never complete
    -- the block.
    --
    -- See: indexer/BlockUploader.kt
}

entity BlockFlusher {
    -- Extracted class that checks whether a block flush timeout has elapsed.
    -- Used by the leader at the start of each processRecords batch.
    -- See: indexer/BlockFlusher.kt
    flush_timeout: Duration
    last_flush_check: Timestamp
    previous_block_tx_id: MessageId
    flushed_tx_id: MessageId
}

external entity StorageFormat {
    -- This node's current storage/buffer_pool format: Storage.VERSION and
    -- BufferPool.epoch. Every TriesAdded/BlockUploaded message carries its own
    -- storage_version/storage_epoch; a mismatch signals a storage upgrade in
    -- progress, and the message's tries are forwarded but not registered locally
    -- until this node upgrades.
    -- See: storage/Storage.kt (VERSION), buffer_pool/BufferPool.kt (epoch)
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
}

---- Values

value MessageId {
    -- Epoch + offset packed into a long.
    -- See: util/MsgIdUtil.kt
    epoch: Integer
    offset: Integer
}

value ExternalSourceToken {
    -- Opaque resume token supplied by an external source (e.g. a Postgres LSN).
    -- Carried through ResolvedTx → BlockBoundary → BlockUploaded on the replica
    -- log, tracked in Watchers, and persisted in Block so that on restart the
    -- external source can resume from the last durably persisted position.
    -- See: api/tx/ExternalSource.kt (ExternalSourceToken = ProtoAny)
}

value TransactionKey {
    -- See: block/proto/block.proto (TxKey)
    -- tx_id is monotonically increasing (assigned by the source log, or off the
    -- RESOLVED head for an external-source database).
    -- system_time is NOT monotonic (clients can set arbitrary system times).
    -- Ordering comparisons MUST use tx_id, not system_time.
    tx_id: Integer
    system_time: Timestamp
}

value TransactionResult {
    -- The outcome of a resolved transaction, carried through the resolver's queue head
    -- and notified to Watchers on apply. Committed carries no error; Aborted carries the
    -- caller-fault or runtime cause.
    -- See: api/TransactionResult.kt
    tx_key: TransactionKey
    committed: Boolean
    error: Throwable?  -- present iff not committed
}

value Block {
    -- A persisted block in the object store.
    block_index: Integer
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey
    latest_processed_msg_id: MessageId         -- source log
    boundary_replica_msg_id: MessageId?        -- replica log; msg id of the BlockBoundary message
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?  -- last token seen by the external source at block time
    -- The term that APPENDED this block's BlockUploaded.
    -- Persisting it is what makes the read-side fence survive a restart: a starting node
    -- seeds max_leader_term from here, so its claims rise above every term written before
    -- the last block (see log-processor-lifecycle.allium, ClaimsRiseAboveWhatWasRead).
    term_id: Integer
    -- Every table this block covers, and where each one's files live. See contract TableEntries.
    tables: List<TableEntry>
    -- See: catalog/TableCatalog.kt, block/proto/block.proto
}

value TableEntry {
    -- A table's identity, and the directory under `tables/` holding its files.
    -- See: table/TableEntry.kt, table/TableSlug.kt, block/proto/block.proto (TableEntry)
    oid: Integer
    table: trie_cat/TableRef
    slug: String
}

value PendingBlock {
    -- Captures the state between BlockBoundary and BlockUploaded.
    -- In the follower: also buffers subsequent replica messages, up to
    -- max_buffered_records — overflow is a fault (xtdb.indexer/follower-buffer-overflow),
    -- which the leader's block-cut pause is what keeps unreachable.
    -- In the leader: set during the upload, cleared after it completes.
    -- Passed from leader to follower on revocation, and from follower to
    -- transition processor on assignment.
    -- See: indexer/PendingBlock.kt
    boundary_msg_id: MessageId
    boundary_message: BlockBoundaryMessage
    buffered_records: List<ReplicaMessage>   -- follower only; leader creates with no buffer
    max_buffered_records: Integer
}

value Snapshot {
    as_of: TransactionKey
}

value TableMetadata {
    row_count: Integer?
}

value DbOp {
    -- A database-level side-effect carried within a ResolvedTx.
    -- See: api/log/DbOp.kt
    kind: Attach | Detach
}

variant Attach : DbOp {
    db_name: String
    config: DatabaseConfig
}

variant Detach : DbOp {
    db_name: String
}

---- Source Log Messages

-- Messages on the source log. Written by clients, the leader (FlushBlock),
-- and the block upload / compactor (TriesAdded).
-- See: log/proto/log.proto (LogMessage oneof), api/log/SourceMessage.kt

entity SourceMessage {
    msg_id: MessageId
    log_timestamp: Timestamp
    kind: TxMessage | LegacyTxMessage | FlushBlockMessage | TriesAddedMessage
         | BlockUploadedMessage | AttachDbMessage | DetachDbMessage
}

variant TxMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- Client-submitted transaction operations.
    -- See: tx/TxWriter.kt, api/log/SourceMessage.kt (SourceMessage$Tx) — the tx ops are
    -- serialised via TxWriter, the envelope via proto.
    system_time: Timestamp?
    default_tz: ZoneId               -- always present (non-nullable)
    user: String?
    user_metadata: Bytes?            -- optional serialized user metadata
}

variant LegacyTxMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- A pre-proto transaction: the whole message is one opaque payload rather than a
    -- proto envelope. Still resolvable (tx_resolver.indexTx has an overload for it) so
    -- that a log written by an older node replays.
    -- See: api/log/SourceMessage.kt (SourceMessage$LegacyTx)
    payload: Bytes
}

variant FlushBlockMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- Leader self-sends to trigger block flush on timeout.
    -- See: log/proto/log.proto (FlushBlock)
    expected_block_idx: Integer?
}

variant TriesAddedMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- New tries available on the source log: L0 posted by the block upload, L1+ written
    -- by the compactor. The leader forwards these to the replica log.
    -- See: log/proto/log.proto (TriesAdded)
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
}

variant BlockUploadedMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- Legacy variant: no current path emits this. The leader ignores it
    -- (notifies watchers only) and it will be removed after 2.2.
    -- See: log/proto/log.proto (BlockUploaded)
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    block_index: Integer
    latest_processed_msg_id: MessageId
    tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
}

variant AttachDbMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- See: log/proto/log.proto (AttachDatabase)
    db_name: String
    config: DatabaseConfig
}

variant DetachDbMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- See: log/proto/log.proto (DetachDatabase)
    db_name: String
}

---- Replica Log Messages

-- Messages on the replica log. Written by the leader for its term, via plain appends.
-- Consumed by all nodes (including the leader itself, on consume-back, and its own
-- follower after transition).

entity ReplicaMessage {
    msg_id: MessageId
    log_timestamp: Timestamp

    -- The leader term that produced this message; 0 (unset) on a record written before
    -- terms existed. Set by the message's CREATOR, not by an append primitive, and
    -- carried on the envelope so every message kind is fenceable. This field is the whole
    -- fence: there is no producer, no fencing token, no write-time failure.
    -- See the Fencing section, log/proto/replica-log.proto (term_id).
    term_id: Integer

    kind: ResolvedTxMessage | BlockBoundaryMessage | BlockUploadedMessage
         | TriesAddedMessage | TriesDeletedMessage | NoOpMessage
}

variant ResolvedTxMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- A resolved transaction: put/delete/erase events that require no database reads.
    -- Attach/detach results are encoded as a dbOp field within the resolved tx.
    -- For external-source databases, carries the ExternalSourceToken (e.g. the
    -- Postgres LSN at the tx boundary) for resume-on-restart.
    -- See: api/log/ReplicaMessage.kt (ResolvedTx)
    tx_id: MessageId                          -- source-log msg id of the original Tx/Attach/Detach,
                                              -- or the resolver-assigned tx_id for external-source DBs
    system_time: Timestamp                    -- resolved system time for the transaction
    committed: Boolean
    error: Throwable?                         -- abort reason (null if committed)
    db_op: DbOp?                              -- Attach or Detach side-effect, if any
    table_data: Map<String, Bytes>            -- resolved row data per table, serialized; imported by replica processor
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?  -- present for external-source DBs

    -- The leader's RESOLVE-side source watermark when this record was produced: for a
    -- source-log tx its own msg_id, for an external-source tx the leader's current
    -- source-log position (so followers keep their durable watermark in step between
    -- block boundaries). Null only on records written before the field existed (#5586),
    -- where readers fall back to the tx_id or the tracked watermark.
    src_msg_id: MessageId?
}

variant BlockBoundaryMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- The leader signals that a block boundary has been reached. Injected by the leader
    -- in RESOLUTION order, off the resolve-side row gauge (or on a FlushBlock CAS pass),
    -- so it lands after this block's txs and before the next block's.
    -- Followers enter pending mode and buffer subsequent messages until BlockUploaded.
    -- L0 tries are delivered in the subsequent BlockUploaded message (not separately).
    -- For external-source DBs, carries the ExternalSourceToken as of the boundary
    -- so it gets persisted in the Block on upload.
    -- See: log/proto/replica-log.proto (BlockBoundary), api/log/ReplicaMessage.kt (BlockBoundary)
    block_index: Integer
    latest_processed_msg_id: MessageId           -- resolve-side source watermark at block time
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?  -- present for external-source DBs
}

variant BlockUploadedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- Block has been written to the object store.
    -- Carries L0 tries so followers can update their trie catalogs.
    -- Followers refresh catalogs and transition out of pending mode.
    -- For external-source DBs, carries the ExternalSourceToken that was
    -- persisted with the block.
    -- See: log/proto/log.proto (BlockUploaded), api/log/ReplicaMessage.kt (BlockUploaded)
    block_index: Integer
    latest_processed_msg_id: MessageId
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?  -- present for external-source DBs
}

variant TriesAddedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- New tries available, forwarded by the leader from the source log.
    -- source_msg_id carries the original source-log position so that staleness
    -- checking and watchers can advance their watermarks correctly.
    -- See: log/proto/log.proto (TriesAdded), api/log/ReplicaMessage.kt (TriesAdded)
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
    source_msg_id: MessageId   -- source-log msg_id of the original TriesAdded
}

variant TriesDeletedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- Tries the leader's trie GC has deleted from the object store, published so that
    -- followers drop them from their in-memory catalogs at the same logical position.
    -- Written only by the leader, only after every file in the chunk is gone. Replay is
    -- always safe — deleteTries is set-removal, so it is idempotent and never stale.
    -- See: gc.allium, api/log/ReplicaMessage.kt (TriesDeleted)
    table_name: String
    trie_keys: Set<trie_cat/TrieKey>
}

variant NoOpMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- No-operation message, used for three things:
    --   1. A leadership claim, stamped one term above the highest the claimant has read.
    --      It confers leadership iff nothing at or above its term precedes it, and the
    --      claimant learns that by reading its own record back — so the claim's position
    --      is the election result, and where the new leader reads on from.
    --   2. Carrying the leader's source watermark forward when no other record would (a
    --      FlushBlock whose CAS fails and so finishes no block) — see #5680.
    --   3. A leader asserting it is still there, at its own term, once the append queue
    --      has been idle for the assert interval — so only the quiet leaders write one.
    --      See log-processor-lifecycle.allium, LiveLeadershipIsVisibleOnTheLog.
    -- See: api/log/ReplicaMessage.kt (NoOp)
    src_msg_id: MessageId?   -- null on a claim
}

---- Config

config {
    -- The worst-case interval over which an external source's upstream must retain its log,
    -- so the value bounds more than block cadence.
    flush_timeout: Duration = 15.minutes
    rows_per_block: Integer = 102400
}

---- Rules: Coordinator Transitions

-- The partition's replica-log reader drives transitions: it cuts over on reading back a claim of its own that conferred, and re-opens a follower on finding the term over.
-- log-processor-lifecycle.allium owns the election and the role state machine.

-- == Fencing ==
--
-- There is at most one CONFIRMED leader per database, enforced by a TERM on the read
-- side. There is no producer and no write-time fence.
--
-- Every replica record carries the term of the leader that created it. A term is taken
-- from the replica log itself: a node claims one above the highest it has read, so the
-- terms come from the same durable record every reader is fenced against.
--
-- The fence bites in two places:
--   - The LEADER, on CONSUME-BACK (Raft's ReadIndex): it applies and acknowledges a write
--     only once it has read that write back at its OWN term. A HIGHER term read back means
--     a newer leader has superseded it, so it RESIGNS without acknowledging its
--     outstanding writes; a LOWER term is discarded, still advancing the consume position.
--   - The PARTITION'S READER, before the record reaches either mode: a record below the
--     highest term it has seen is DISCARDED, its consume position still advancing (so a
--     reader catching up never stalls on a fenced no-op). Its high-water term is seeded
--     from the last persisted block, so the fence survives restart.
--
-- Two nodes may therefore briefly both believe themselves leader and both APPEND. Only
-- the newer term's writes are ever confirmed, and the older leader's are discarded, which
-- is the property that matters — not single-writer-at-the-log.
--
-- Term 0 (unset) is never fenced, so a mixed-version window doesn't discard a
-- not-yet-upgraded leader's writes.
--
-- The SOURCE log is not fenced. Stale messages from a previous leader (FlushBlock) or the
-- compactor (TriesAdded) may appear after a transition. This is safe because:
--   - FlushBlock is a CAS on expected_block_idx; stale flushes fail the check.
--   - TriesAdded is idempotent; addTries tolerates duplicates.

-- == Transition Safety ==
--
-- Teardown ABANDONS in-flight resolution rather than draining it. A staged-but-not-yet-
-- applied tx was never visible to an external query, so discarding it costs nothing: the
-- next leader re-reads it from the source log and re-resolves it. What teardown MUST do
-- instead is fail everything awaiting the term — every staged external-source tx's
-- durability handle, the paused source batch, and whatever is still queued — because the
-- symptom of missing one is a HANG, and for a source batch that hang is on the tail that feeds the term (#5711 / #5817).
--
-- So the live index still reflects a complete prefix of the replica log: it is only ever
-- advanced by APPLY, one whole tx at a time, and an abandoned tx is one that was never
-- applied.

-- == Leader Startup ==
--
-- A new leader can always reconstruct correct state from:
--   1. The last persisted block (from the table catalog)
--   2. The follower's in-memory state (which has been processing replica messages)
--
-- The follower is fully caught up by construction when its state is captured: the claim is read and applied in its own position by the same reader, so nothing below the claim can still be outstanding.
-- If the follower had a pending block (saw BlockBoundary but not BlockUploaded), the new
-- leader finishes the block via BlockUploader — under its OWN term — before starting
-- source log consumption.

-- == Staleness ==
--
-- The follower skips a replica message whose effect has already been applied.
-- Each kind is compared against the watermark that actually records its effect, rather than against a single source-log position:
--   ResolvedTx:     stale if tx_id <= watchers.latest_tx_id
--   TriesAdded:     stale if source_msg_id <= watchers.latest_source_msg_id
--   BlockBoundary:  stale if block_index <= table_catalog.current_block_index
--   BlockUploaded:  stale if block_index <= table_catalog.current_block_index
--   NoOp:           stale if it carries a src_msg_id at or behind
--                   watchers.latest_source_msg_id; a bare marker NoOp is never stale
--   TriesDeleted:   never stale — deleteTries is set-removal, so replay is a no-op
-- Stale messages are skipped entirely before dispatch. This replaces per-type
-- idempotency checks within the handler.
--
-- The term fence is a SEPARATE, earlier check (see Fencing): a fenced record is discarded
-- before staleness is even considered.

rule TransitionToLeader {
    -- db.allium's view of the cut-over: what it means for THIS database's pipeline.
    -- log-processor-lifecycle.allium owns the claim, its adjudication and the recovery path; don't duplicate them here.
    when: ClaimConferredLeadership(database, claim_pos, term)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower

    ensures:
        -- The claim was applied like any other record before it conferred, so there is nothing left to catch up to and no term to refuse.
        -- A claim carries a term above everything this node has read, and a claim that anything at or above its term precedes confers nothing.

        -- Close the follower system. No further processing after this point.
        log_processor.close()

        -- Capture the follower's pending block state (if it saw BlockBoundary
        -- but not BlockUploaded before the cut-over).
        let pending_block = log_processor.pending_block

        -- Switch to leader mode.
        -- It resumes the SOURCE log from the durable watermark (which the replay below advances), and reads the REPLICA log back from its claim.
        -- Its resolver is seeded at the durable head, and its block gauge from the rows already in the open block, so it cuts this block where the previous leader would have.
        log_processor.mode = Leader.created(
            -- The database's own source, borrowed for the length of this term, so this leader is CDC-fed iff the database is.
            external_source: database.external_source,
            leader_term: term,
            latest_replica_msg_id: claim_pos,
            last_resolved_src_msg_id: watchers.latest_source_msg_id,
            last_resolved_ext_token: watchers.external_source_token,
            rows_since_block: live_index.block_row_count,
            block_in_progress: false,
            pending_block: null
        )
        TxResolver.created(
            latest_completed_tx: live_index.latest_completed_tx
        )

        -- The new leader finishes the block the outgoing one left behind, before it reads a source message.
        --   - The block's data is already in the live index (applied before BlockBoundary), but the block file was never persisted.
        --   - The BlockUploaded is appended under the INCOMING term, not the term that cut the boundary: followers have already advanced past that term and would fence it, never completing the block.
        --   - The buffered records then replay through this same leader, which uploads on a BlockBoundary among them rather than entering the follower's pending mode.
        if pending_block != null:
            block_uploader.uploadBlock(
                pending_block.boundary_msg_id,
                term,
                pending_block.boundary_message
            )
            log_processor.applyPendingBlock(pending_block)

        log_processor.start(after_source: watchers.latest_source_msg_id, after_replica: claim_pos)

    @guidance
        -- ReplayEquivalence: the live index state after transition is identical
        -- regardless of how much work the follower had already done.
}

rule TransitionToFollower {
    when: LeaderTermEnded(database)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- Two-phase close. Phase 1: cancelAndJoin the leader's job tree (resolver +
        -- append pump + source-log tail + GCs) so nothing live still touches the resolver.
        -- On the resolver's exit it FAILS every pending durability handle before the
        -- channels close, so no executeTx caller waits forever (live-index
        -- FailPendingHandles).
        log_processor.cancelAndJoin()

        -- Phase 2: synchronously free the resolver — the buffers of any staged-but-not-
        -- yet-applied tx — before the allocator. Demotion IS this teardown; there is no
        -- separate runtime drop. Those txs are discarded (they were never visible to
        -- external queries) and will be re-read from the source log and re-resolved by the
        -- next leader. The durable live index is untouched (live-index CloseResolver).
        log_processor.close()
        if tx_resolver != null:
            CloseResolver(tx_resolver)

        -- The leader's pending_block is passed to the new follower. If the leader was
        -- mid-upload when its term ended (between BlockBoundary and BlockUploaded), the follower
        -- picks up the pending state. The watermark fields stay readable after the close —
        -- they are not allocator-backed.
        log_processor.mode = Follower.created(
            latest_replica_msg_id: log_processor.latest_replica_msg_id,
            pending_block: log_processor.pending_block,
            max_leader_term: table_catalog.boundary_term_id
        )
}

---- Rules: Transaction Submission

rule ClientSubmitsTransaction {
    -- Client submits a transaction: it is appended to the SOURCE log, which every node
    -- shares, so this succeeds whether or not this node happens to be the leader.
    -- See: database/Database.kt (submitTxBlocking)
    when: ClientSubmitsTx(client, database, tx_ops, opts)

    requires: database.external_source = null

    ensures:
        let msg = TxMessage.created(
            system_time: opts.system_time,
            default_tz: opts.default_tz,
            user: opts.user,
            user_metadata: opts.user_metadata
        )
        msg appended to database.source_log
}

rule ClientSubmitsTransactionToExternalSourceDb {
    -- Rejected: submitTx is not a valid entry point for an external-source DB, which
    -- assigns its own tx_id sequence off the resolved head and accepts transactions only
    -- via the TxIndexer callback. Decided from the DATABASE's config, not this node's
    -- role — see Database.external_source.
    when: ClientSubmitsTx(client, database, tx_ops, opts)

    requires: database.external_source != null

    ensures:
        SubmitTxRejected(
            error_code: `xtdb/submit-tx-to-external-source-db`,
            db_name: database.name
        )
}

---- Rules: Leader Mode — resolve side

-- The leader's persister select loop is the RESOLVER. Its arms are the source-log batch
-- pipe, the external-source task channel, the GC task channel, the block-cut resume, and
-- the consume-back channel (the APPLY arm, further down). All but the apply arm are
-- EXCLUDED from the select while block_in_progress — the block-cut pause.
--
-- Resolving a tx stages it on the resolver's own queue (advancing the RESOLVED head, so
-- later txs read-your-writes against it) and SENDS it to the append pump. Nothing here
-- touches the durable live index and nothing here notifies watchers: both follow from
-- apply, on consume-back. Resolution therefore runs AHEAD of durability.
--
-- At the start of each source batch the leader checks the block flush timeout
-- (BlockFlusher). A batch that a block cut pauses part-way is STASHED and resumed after
-- the upload, completing its handle only once fully drained — so the source-log tail stays in step with resolution.

rule LeaderProcessesTx {
    -- Covers TxMessage and LegacyTxMessage alike: they differ only in how the payload
    -- decodes, which is below this spec's abstraction level.
    when: SourceTxMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- Resolve and stage on the resolver's queue, advancing the RESOLVED head
        -- (live-index CommitTransaction / AbortTransaction — an aborted tx is staged too,
        -- as an abort row, and still consumes a tx_id).
        let resolved = ResolveTx(tx_resolver, msg)
        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: msg.msg_id)
}

rule LeaderProcessesAttachDb {
    -- The leader asks whether the attach would be refused, and brings its own set into line
    -- when it reads the record back, as every other node does. A term superseded between
    -- resolving an attach and consuming it back has therefore attached nothing.
    --
    -- The verdict accounts for what this term has already resolved as well as for the
    -- catalog, which shows the state before every dbOp still queued on the resolver. The
    -- last one queued for a name says what will be there when this attach applies; with
    -- none, the catalog's own state stands. This is the layering a resolving transaction
    -- reads its own queued predecessors through, turned on the catalog rather than on
    -- tables.
    --
    -- A caller fault is resolved as a FAILED dbOp tx rather than propagating: the
    -- transaction aborts with that error, and the log carries the abort.
    when: SourceAttachDbMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        let error = CheckCanAttach(msg.db_name, msg.config)
        let resolved =
            if error = null:
                ResolveDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, Attach{db_name: msg.db_name, config: msg.config})
            else:
                ResolveFailedDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, error)
        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: msg.msg_id)
}

rule LeaderProcessesDetachDb {
    -- Mirror of LeaderProcessesAttachDb.
    when: SourceDetachDbMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        let error = CheckCanDetach(msg.db_name)
        let resolved =
            if error = null:
                ResolveDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, Detach{db_name: msg.db_name})
            else:
                ResolveFailedDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, error)
        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: msg.msg_id)
}

rule AppendTx {
    -- Hand a freshly-resolved tx to the append pump, advance the resolve-side watermark,
    -- and cut a block if this tx filled one.
    --
    -- src_msg_id is the source-log position this tx sits at: a source-log tx advances it;
    -- an external-source tx passes the current one back, having no source-log position of
    -- its own. Either way it is what gets STAMPED on the tx's replica record — see the
    -- header's note on the two watermarks.
    when: AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id)

    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = src_msg_id
        log_processor.rows_since_block = log_processor.rows_since_block + resolved.row_count
        AppendReplicaMessage(resolved)

        if log_processor.rows_since_block >= config.rows_per_block:
            LeaderCutsBlock(log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id, log_processor.last_resolved_ext_token)
}

rule LeaderCutsBlock {
    -- Inject the boundary in RESOLUTION order (so it lands after this block's txs and
    -- before the next block's) and PAUSE resolution until it is read back and its block
    -- uploaded. The pause is what keeps the follower's bounded pending-block buffer empty.
    -- Deliberately the status quo — block double-buffering is separate work (#4495).
    --
    -- The same event as live-index.allium's CutBlock, from the db side: this is where the
    -- boundary MESSAGE is built, because its fields come from here (the next block index
    -- off the table catalog, the resolve-side source watermark, the resume token) —
    -- live-index holds neither the resolve-side watermark nor the external source.
    --
    -- One rule, not one per leader flavour: the token is whatever the resolve side last
    -- saw (null throughout for a client-driven leader), so there is nothing to branch on.
    -- Reading the token from the WATCHERS instead would be wrong here — they advance on
    -- apply, which lags this cut.
    when: LeaderCutsBlock(latest_processed_msg_id, external_source_token)

    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    let block_index = (table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1) + 1

    ensures:
        AppendReplicaMessage(BlockBoundaryMessage.created(
            block_index: block_index,
            latest_processed_msg_id: latest_processed_msg_id,
            external_source_token: external_source_token
        ))
        log_processor.block_in_progress = true
        log_processor.rows_since_block = 0
}

rule LeaderProcessesFlushBlock {
    -- Source-log coordination message, handled on the resolver.
    when: SourceFlushBlockMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- CAS check: only cut the block if the expected index matches.
        if msg.expected_block_idx != null
           and msg.expected_block_idx = (table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1):
            LeaderCutsBlock(msg.msg_id, log_processor.last_resolved_ext_token)
        else:
            -- Stale FlushBlock (or a null expected index): nothing to cut, but the source
            -- watermark still has to reach the followers, and no other record would carry
            -- it. A NoOp does (#5680).
            AppendReplicaMessage(NoOpMessage.created(src_msg_id: msg.msg_id))

        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = msg.msg_id
}

rule LeaderProcessesTriesAdded {
    -- L0 from the block upload, L1+ from the compactor. The leader needs these in its own
    -- trie catalog for correct partition info at block time, and forwards them to the
    -- replica log for followers.
    --
    -- The catalog mutation is EAGER, on the resolve side, rather than deferred to the
    -- leader's own consume-back: callers read the catalog synchronously (the compactor and
    -- GC do), and it stays a projection of the fenced log anyway, because the block-cut
    -- pause serialises trie mutations against boundaries — so no block snapshot straddles
    -- an add. Skipped on the leader's own consume-back; the follower applies it there.
    when: SourceTriesAddedMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        if msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
           and msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
            trie_cat/trie_catalog.addTries(msg.tries, msg.log_timestamp)

        -- Forward regardless of storage version: the follower applies the same guard, and
        -- the message still has to carry the source watermark forward.
        AppendReplicaMessage(TriesAddedMessage.created(
            storage_version: msg.storage_version,
            storage_epoch: msg.storage_epoch,
            tries: msg.tries,
            source_msg_id: msg.msg_id
        ))

        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = msg.msg_id
}

-- The trie GC's publish step is a THIRD resolve-side arm, alongside the source log and the
-- external source: the GC hands `commitTriesDeleted` to the persister and awaits it, rather
-- than touching the catalog or the log itself. gc.allium owns that rule
-- (CommitTriesDeletedRule), including why the catalog removal and the replica-log append
-- need no lock. Two facts belong here, being properties of this pipeline rather than of the
-- GC:
--   - the GC arm is EXCLUDED from the select while block_in_progress, so a TriesDeleted can
--     never be resolved between a boundary and its upload;
--   - its TriesDeletedMessage leaves via the append pump like any other, and is skipped on
--     the leader's own consume-back — the catalog was already mutated on the resolve side.

rule LeaderProcessesBlockUploaded {
    -- Legacy variant: no current path emits BlockUploaded on the source log. The leader
    -- ignores it, advancing the watermarks directly — including the resolve-side one, or a
    -- following block cut would carry a lower latest_processed_msg_id and regress
    -- notifyMsg on apply.
    when: SourceBlockUploadedMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        watchers.notifyMsg(msg.msg_id)
        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = msg.msg_id
}

---- Rules: Leader Mode — append pump

rule AppendReplicaMessage {
    -- The append pump: serialise the message (for a tx, the costly Arrow-IPC step, kept
    -- off the resolver's hot path) and append it to the replica log, in resolution order,
    -- STAMPED with this leader's term.
    --
    -- A PLAIN append. Nothing here is atomic across messages and nothing needs to be: a
    -- superseded leader is fenced by the term its records carry, checked when it reads
    -- them back (#5817), not by an append that fails. An append fault propagates and tears
    -- the term down.
    --
    -- The pump only BORROWS a tx to serialise it — the resolver owns and frees it (see
    -- live-index.allium "Ownership").
    when: AppendReplicaMessage(msg)

    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        msg.term_id = log_processor.leader_term
        msg appended to database.replica_log
}

---- Rules: Leader Mode — consume-back / apply (ReadIndex)

-- The partition's replica-log reader hands each record to whichever role is live, so on a leader it is the leader reading its own writes back.
-- Records reach the term over a bounded channel that the resolver's select loop services.
-- Because resolution and apply share one coroutine, a tx cannot interleave a partial state.
--
-- This is where a committed tx becomes queryable and where an external-source caller's
-- durability handle completes: confirmed durably replicated AND confirmed unfenced.

rule LeaderAppliesRecord {
    -- Apply one record read back off our own replica log, fenced by TERM. Consumption
    -- advances in all three branches — discarding suppresses application, not consumption,
    -- so a catch-up can never hang on a fenced record.
    when: ReplicaMessageReceived(record)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        if record.term_id > log_processor.leader_term:
            -- A newer leader has superseded us. RESIGN: the term fails without
            -- acknowledging its outstanding writes, its teardown fails everything awaiting
            -- it (see Transition Safety and live-index.allium FailPendingHandles), and the
            -- reader re-opens a follower when it finds the term over.
            --
            -- A clean resignation, NOT an ingestion fault: it must not poison the watchers,
            -- or a routine leadership handover would surface to queries as a failed
            -- database (see ProcessingErrorHaltsNode).
            LeaderSuperseded(resolver: tx_resolver)
        else if record.term_id != 0 and record.term_id < log_processor.leader_term:
            -- A term below ours, which the partition's reader has already fenced; discard defensively.
            RecordDiscarded(resolver: tx_resolver)
            log_processor.latest_replica_msg_id = record.msg_id
        else:
            -- Equal term (the common case): terms are unique per leader, so this record is
            -- our own.
            ApplyOwnRecord(record)
            log_processor.latest_replica_msg_id = record.msg_id
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnTx {
    -- The leader reads its own staged tx back at its own term and IMPORTS it. It imports
    -- from the resolver's QUEUE HEAD — which still holds the tx's relations, so there is
    -- NO re-materialisation from the replica record; that record's only job was to confirm
    -- durability and term. A head whose tx_id doesn't match the record just read back is a
    -- fault.
    --
    -- This is the point at which the tx becomes queryable: the DURABLE head advances here,
    -- watchers are notified here, and an external-source caller's durability handle
    -- completes here — after, not before, the tx is durable.
    --
    -- The dbOp (attach/detach) it carries is applied here, as a follower applies it on its
    -- own consume-back — before the durability handle completes, so a caller that submitted
    -- an attach can use the database the moment its transaction returns.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = ResolvedTxMessage

    -- Take the head FIRST, then check it. Taking it first is what makes the failure path
    -- safe: once off the queue, teardown's fail-pending can no longer reach it, so this
    -- rule owns failing its durability handle and freeing its buffers on ANY throw here —
    -- the tx_id mismatch included.
    let head = TakeResolvedHead(tx_resolver)
    requires: head.tx_key.tx_id = record.tx_id

    ensures:
        -- Everything notified below comes from the HEAD, not from the record: the head still
        -- holds the tx's result, watermark stamp and token, which is exactly why the record
        -- needs no re-materialisation. The two agree by construction — the head is what was
        -- stamped onto the record at append time.
        ApplyResolvedTx(tx_resolver, head)
        if record.committed and record.db_op != null:
            ApplyDbOpToCatalog(database.catalog, record.db_op)
        watchers.notifyTx(head.tx_result, head.src_msg_id, head.external_source_token)
}

rule LeaderUploadsBlockOnBoundary {
    -- The leader reads its OWN BlockBoundary back. By log order the durable live index now
    -- holds exactly this block's txs and no more — which is precisely what the block-cut
    -- pause buys: nothing was resolved-and-applied between the boundary and here, so a
    -- durable L0 block never contains rows from a later block.
    --
    -- uploadBlock snapshots the index, uploads the files, appends BlockUploaded and rolls
    -- the index. Then the GCs are signalled (a block boundary is the only moment new
    -- garbage can appear) and resolution RESUMES, picking up any stashed source batch.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = BlockBoundaryMessage

    ensures:
        log_processor.pending_block = PendingBlock(
            boundary_msg_id: record.msg_id,
            boundary_message: record
        )
        block_uploader.uploadBlock(record.msg_id, log_processor.leader_term, record)
        log_processor.pending_block = null

        -- uploadBlock has returned, so this block's files are in object storage. Announcing
        -- that as its own event is what lets an external source confirm its upstream
        -- position without reaching into the uploader.
        BlockBecameDurable(database, record.external_source_token)

        watchers.notifyMsg(record.latest_processed_msg_id)

        BlockGcSignalled()
        TrieGcSignalled()

        log_processor.block_in_progress = false
        ResolutionResumed()
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnControlRecord {
    -- The leader's remaining own records need no work on apply — only the durable
    -- watermark advance, which is exactly what makes it lag resolution.
    --   TriesAdded / TriesDeleted: the trie catalog was already mutated on the resolve side.
    --   BlockUploaded: our own, read back after uploadBlock already rolled the index.
    --   NoOp: advances the watermark iff it carries one (a replay-target marker doesn't).
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind in {TriesAddedMessage, TriesDeletedMessage, BlockUploadedMessage, NoOpMessage}

    ensures:
        if record.kind = TriesAddedMessage:
            watchers.notifyMsg(record.source_msg_id)
        if record.kind = BlockUploadedMessage:
            watchers.notifyMsg(record.latest_processed_msg_id)
        if record.kind = NoOpMessage and record.src_msg_id != null:
            watchers.notifyMsg(record.src_msg_id)
}

---- Rules: Leader Flush Timeout

rule LeaderFlushTimeout {
    -- BlockFlusher checks at the start of each source batch. It compares the current
    -- block's latest tx_id against the previous check; if the block hasn't advanced after
    -- flush_timeout, and we haven't already flushed this tx, the leader writes FlushBlock
    -- to the SOURCE log — so the cut goes through resolution in log order like any other
    -- boundary trigger, rather than being applied out of band.
    -- See: indexer/BlockFlusher.kt
    -- BlockFlushTimeoutElapsed models the periodic check going positive: not a log
    -- delivery, so it is its own event rather than a per-message-type one.
    when: BlockFlushTimeoutElapsed(database)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    requires: live_index.has_data

    ensures:
        FlushBlockMessage.created(
            expected_block_idx: table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1
        ) appended to database.source_log
}

---- Rules: External Source

-- An external-source leader does NOT take transactions from the source log. The external
-- source is handed a TxIndexer callback on partition assignment and feeds transactions in
-- directly. Flush/tries/block-uploaded coordination still uses the source log, so the
-- leader also reads it — but TxMessage / AttachDatabase / DetachDatabase MUST NOT appear
-- there, rejected at submit time (ClientSubmitsTransactionToExternalSourceDb).
--
-- See: indexer/LeaderLogProcessor.kt (the TxIndexer half), api/tx/ExternalSource.kt

rule ExternalSourceAssigned {
    -- On partition assignment the external source receives a TxIndexer and the resume
    -- token to start after, then begins streaming txs. The token comes from
    -- watchers.external_source_token, which reflects the replay of the replica log and so
    -- may be ahead of the last persisted block.
    -- A node that leads, is demoted, then leads again is assigned again on the same source instance, so state belonging to one assignment lives inside the call rather than on the source.
    when: PartitionsAssigned(database)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader
    requires: database.external_source != null

    ensures:
        log_processor.external_source.onPartitionAssigned(
            partition: 0,
            after_token: watchers.external_source_token,
            tx_indexer: TxIndexer
        )
}

rule ExternalSourceIndexesTx {
    -- Per-tx entry point. The external source calls TxIndexer.executeTx (awaits the
    -- result) or TxIndexer.submitTx (fire-and-forget, returning the handle) with an opaque
    -- resume token, an optional system_time, and a writer that populates an OpenTx with
    -- the transformed row data. The per-tx indexing below is identical for both; they
    -- differ only in whether the caller awaits, which is caller-side.
    --
    -- Unlike a client-driven tx, tx_id here is assigned off the RESOLVED head
    -- (tx_resolver.latest_completed_tx.tx_id + 1), NOT a source-log position and NOT the
    -- durable head. system_time is smoothed strictly greater than the previous tx's.
    --
    -- The caller's handle completes on CONSUME-BACK (LeaderAppliesOwnTx), so it means
    -- durably replicated AND confirmed unfenced. Every path on which the tx will never be
    -- applied MUST fail it instead, or the caller waits forever — see live-index.allium
    -- "Per-tx durability handles".
    --
    -- Backpressure is the task channel's capacity (one queued tx of lookahead) plus the
    -- block-cut pause: while a block is in progress the external-source arm is excluded
    -- from the select, so a bursty source cannot grow the resolver's queue without bound.
    when: ExternalSourceIndexTx(external_source_token, system_time, writer)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader
    requires: database.external_source != null

    ensures:
        -- The stamped watermark MUST be the resolve-side one, not watchers': a source tx
        -- resolved and appended ahead of this one applies first and pushes the durable
        -- watermark past a stale stamp, at which point notifyTx's monotonicity check fires
        -- (#5817).
        let resolved = ResolveExternalSourceTx(
            tx_resolver, system_time, external_source_token, writer,
            src_msg_id: log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id
        )

        if external_source_token != null:
            log_processor.last_resolved_ext_token = external_source_token

        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id)
}

---- Rules: Replica Message Handling

-- A follower and the transition processor both apply already-durable messages, so they
-- import straight into the durable live index and advance the watchers as they go. Both
-- run two checks before dispatch:
--   1. The TERM FENCE (see Fencing): a record below the highest term seen is discarded,
--      its consume position still advancing.
--   2. STALENESS (see Staleness): a message whose effect is already applied is skipped.
--
-- In the follower, staleness is checked OUTSIDE the pending block guard: if in pending
-- mode, the pending block handler runs first (buffers, or matches BlockUploaded). Only
-- non-pending, non-stale messages reach processRecord.

-- == Follower handleRecord dispatch ==
--
-- 1. Pending block guard: if pending_block is set, check whether the message is the
--    matching BlockUploaded. If so, finish the block and replay the buffer. Otherwise
--    buffer it (overflowing the cap is a fault). Return in either case.
-- 2. Staleness check: if the message is stale, skip. Otherwise processRecord.

rule ReaderFencesRecord {
    -- The read-side fence, folded by the partition's reader before either mode sees the record.
    --   Term 0 is never fenced, so a not-yet-upgraded leader's writes still apply.
    --   A discard advances the consume position: it suppresses application, not consumption.
    when: ReplicaMessageReceived(record)

    ensures:
        if record.term_id != 0 and record.term_id < log_processor.max_leader_term:
            RecordDiscarded(record)
        else:
            log_processor.max_leader_term = max(log_processor.max_leader_term, record.term_id)
            HandleReplicaMessage(record)

        -- Advanced after every record, discarded or not, and on error too — a role change continues from it.
        log_processor.latest_replica_msg_id = record.msg_id
}

rule HandleReplicaMessage {
    -- Dispatches a single replica message that has passed the fence, the pending-block
    -- guard and the staleness check. The transition processor overrides BlockBoundary
    -- (see Transition rules below).
    when: HandleReplicaMessage(msg)

    ensures:
        if msg.kind = ResolvedTxMessage:
            live_index.importTx(msg)
            if msg.committed:
                if msg.db_op.kind = Attach:
                    database.catalog.attach(msg.db_op.db_name, msg.db_op.config)
                if msg.db_op.kind = Detach:
                    database.catalog.detach(msg.db_op.db_name)
            -- src_msg_id where present. On a pre-#5586 record it is absent, and the
            -- fallback differs by database: an external-source tx_id is an independent
            -- counter, so it says nothing about the source log and the tracked watermark
            -- has to stand in; a source-log tx_id IS a source-log position.
            let effective_src_msg_id =
                msg.src_msg_id
                    ?? (if database.external_source != null:
                            watchers.latest_source_msg_id
                        else: msg.tx_id)
            watchers.notifyTx(msg.result, effective_src_msg_id, msg.external_source_token)

        if msg.kind = TriesAddedMessage:
            if msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
               and msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
                trie_cat/trie_catalog.addTries(msg.tries, msg.log_timestamp)
            watchers.notifyMsg(msg.source_msg_id)

        if msg.kind = TriesDeletedMessage:
            -- Set-removal, so unconditional and idempotent: no storage-version guard and
            -- no staleness check.
            trie_cat/trie_catalog.deleteTries(msg.table_name, msg.trie_keys)

        if msg.kind = BlockBoundaryMessage:
            -- Follower: enters pending mode. Subsequent messages (except the matching
            -- BlockUploaded) are buffered by the pending block guard above.
            log_processor.pending_block = PendingBlock(msg.msg_id, msg)
            watchers.notifyMsg(msg.latest_processed_msg_id)

        if msg.kind = NoOpMessage and msg.src_msg_id != null:
            watchers.notifyMsg(msg.src_msg_id)
}

rule FollowerHandlesBlockUploaded {
    -- When the follower is in pending mode and receives the matching BlockUploaded
    -- message, it finishes the block by reading the block file from the object store (via
    -- buffer pool), not from the message. The BlockUploaded message is just a signal.
    -- Subscribes to the same dispatch event as HandleReplicaMessage (which has no
    -- BlockUploadedMessage branch of its own) and overrides it for the pending-block case.
    when: HandleReplicaMessage(msg)
    requires: msg.kind = BlockUploadedMessage
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower
    requires: log_processor.pending_block != null
    requires: msg.block_index = log_processor.pending_block.boundary_message.block_index
    requires: msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
    requires: msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch

    ensures:
        trie_cat/trie_catalog.addTries(msg.tries, msg.log_timestamp)

        -- Read the block file from the object store.
        let block = Block.parseFrom(buffer_pool.getByteArray(blockFilePath(msg.block_index)))
        table_catalog.refresh(block, live_index.blockMetadata())
        live_index.nextBlock()
        CompactorSignalled()

        -- Clear pending mode and replay buffered records; their typed notifications are
        -- what advance the watermarks.
        let buffered = log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records
        log_processor.pending_block = null
        for record in buffered:
            HandleReplicaMessage(record)
}

---- Rules: Compaction Orchestration

-- The compactor runs asynchronously, triggered after block flushes.
-- Job selection and the merge algorithm are specified in compaction.allium.
-- The compactor writes L1+ TriesAdded to the source log (not the replica log).
-- The leader forwards these to the replica log.

rule CompactorWakesUp {
    when: CompactorSignalled()

    let jobs = compaction/available_jobs(trie_cat/trie_catalog)

    ensures:
        for job in jobs:
            StartCompactionJob(job)
}

rule CompactionJobWritesOutput {
    when: StartCompactionJob(job)

    ensures:
        -- Data file uploaded first; meta file presence is the completion marker.

        -- Write TriesAdded to the source log.
        -- The leader will forward to the replica log.
        TriesAddedMessage.created(tries: job.output_tries) appended to database.source_log
}

rule CompactionJobConfirmed {
    -- The TriesAdded has round-tripped through the log and is now in the trie catalog.
    -- Job selection naturally sees the updated catalog for the next cycle, so this rule
    -- asserts the event and adds no postconditions of its own.
    -- CompactionOutputVisible models the catalog visibility check going positive: not a
    -- log delivery, so it is its own event rather than a per-message-type one.
    when: CompactionOutputVisible(job)
}

---- Rules: Query Serving

rule ClientOpensSnapshot {
    -- External queries open at the DURABLE head: latest_completed_tx, never the resolved
    -- head. A query must never see a tx that could vanish if the leader crashed before its
    -- replica-log write landed, nor one a newer-term leader superseded.
    when: ClientOpensSnapshot(client, database)

    ensures:
        Snapshot.created(
            as_of: live_index.latest_completed_tx
        )
}

rule ExternalSourceLearnsDurableExtent {
    -- Confirming a resume token tells the upstream it may discard everything behind it, so a
    -- confirmed position is a claim that we can recover without it. Only a durable block
    -- backs that claim: between apply and the next block the sole copies are the replica log
    -- and the leader's live index, and the upstream cannot re-send what we told it to drop.
    --
    -- So this token, and not the applied position, is the source's licence to confirm. It is
    -- also not watchers.external_source_token, which is the one that may run ahead —
    -- replica-log replay advances it past block boundaries — which is why
    -- ExternalSourceAssigned's after_token is not a position we are entitled to confirm.
    --
    -- What a source does with the licence is its own rule: it may confirm further where the
    -- log above the extent demonstrably holds nothing we would need. See pgsrc.allium.
    --
    -- The gap this opens is the block cadence: a database whose blocks stop landing pins the
    -- upstream's log while ingestion and queries stay healthy. That is a monitoring
    -- obligation, not a correctness one (#5867).
    when: BlockBecameDurable(database, external_source_token)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader
    requires: database.external_source != null
    requires: external_source_token != null

    ensures:
        log_processor.external_source.durableExtentAdvanced(external_source_token)
}

---- Rules: Error Handling

rule ProcessingErrorHaltsNode {
    -- A genuine ingestion fault: watchers.notifyError transitions Watchers to Failed, and
    -- all subsequent awaitTx/awaitSource calls throw.
    --
    -- A SUPERSESSION is not one of these, and neither is a cancellation or an interrupt:
    -- see LeaderSuperseded. Failed is absorbing, so poisoning the watchers on a routine
    -- leadership handover would leave a database that is still fully readable answering
    -- every query with a fault, for as long as the process lives.
    when: ProcessingError(msg_id, error)

    ensures:
        watchers.notifyError(error)
        NodeMarkedUnhealthy()
}

---- Contracts

contract TableEntries {
    -- Obligations on the entries a Block carries: each table's identity, and where its files live.
    -- These are about an entry's history across successive blocks, so they cannot be expressed as single-snapshot invariants over entity state.

    @invariant SlugFixedWhenFirstRecorded
        -- A table's slug is chosen once, by the first block that records the table, and every later block carries it forward unchanged.

    @invariant OidFixedWhenTheTableIsFirstSeen
        -- A table's oid is allocated when the catalog first learns of the table, and every later block carries it forward unchanged.
        -- - The catalog learns of a table when a transaction writes to it, which is earlier than the block that first records it.

    @invariant EveryNodeAllocatesTheSameOid
        -- Two nodes reach the same oid for a table without it passing between them.
        -- - Allocation follows the order tables first appear in the log, and the tables of one transaction are allocated in name order, so replaying the same log is enough to agree.

    @invariant EveryTableTheBlockNamesHasAnEntry
        -- A block records an entry for each table it names, taking the entry from the preceding block wherever that block held one.

    @invariant UnrecordedTableTakesItsEscapedName
        -- A table a block does not record takes the escaped form of its name as its slug.
        -- - This is what a block carrying names alone resolves to, so the files its tables have already written stay where they are.
        -- - Oids for those tables are derived in name order, so every node resolving the same block resolves the same entries.

    @invariant SlugPrecedesTheFirstWrite
        -- A table's slug is fixed before anything is written under its path.
        -- - A live table takes its entry when the live index creates it, so the L0 trie it writes at the block boundary lands under the slug the block then records for it.

    @invariant OnlyARecordedTableHasABlockFile
        -- A per-table block file exists for exactly the tables the block records.
        -- - A table allocated but not yet written has an entry and no file.
}

contract ExternalSourceConfirmation {
    -- Obligations on an ExternalSource that confirms progress upstream. These are about the
    -- ordering of confirmations against block durability over time, so they cannot be
    -- expressed as single-snapshot invariants over entity state.

    @invariant ConfirmedPositionIsRecoverable
        -- Everything the upstream has been told it may discard is recoverable without it, so
        -- recovery is the last block plus the upstream resumed from the token that block
        -- carries — one artefact, not two.
        -- - Ordinarily this bounds the confirmed position by the last durable block's token.
        --   A source MAY confirm beyond it over a range that holds nothing of ours; that is
        --   the source's rule to state and to justify, per pgsrc.allium.

    @invariant ConfirmationNeverNamesAnUnappliedTransaction
        -- No confirmed position covers a transaction that has not been applied.
        -- - Below the durable extent this holds by construction rather than by a check: a
        --   BlockBoundary is appended after the transactions it covers, so by the time a
        --   block is durable every transaction its token names has already been applied.
        --   The gate can therefore only hold confirmation back, never push it past apply.
        -- - Above the extent it holds because the only positions a source may confirm there
        --   are ones it has established name no transaction at all.

    @invariant ConfirmationIsLeaderOnly
        -- Only the leader for a database holds its ExternalSource, so only the leader
        -- confirms. A follower has no upstream connection and no position of its own — it
        -- reads watchers.latest_source_msg_id — so a leadership handover cannot produce two
        -- nodes confirming different positions to the same upstream.
}

---- Actor Declarations

actor ClientActor {
    -- Minimal identification: any Client is a valid actor for the surfaces below.
    identified_by: Client where true
}

---- Surfaces

surface TransactionSubmission {
    facing caller: ClientActor

    context db: Database

    provides:
        ClientSubmitsTx(caller, db, tx_ops, opts)
            when db.external_source = null

    @guarantee OrderedProcessing
        -- Transactions are processed in the order they appear in the source log.

    @guarantee SubmitIsRoleAgnostic
        -- Submission appends to the shared source log, so it succeeds from any node
        -- regardless of which node leads this database. Whether the transaction has been
        -- INDEXED is a separate question, answered by awaiting its txId on the watchers.
}

surface QueryAccess {
    facing caller: ClientActor

    context db: Database

    provides:
        ClientOpensSnapshot(caller, db)

    @guarantee SnapshotIsolation
        -- A snapshot sees a consistent point-in-time view.
        -- It includes all transactions up to latest_completed_tx — those the leader has
        -- read BACK off its own replica log and applied. Resolved-but-not-yet-applied txs
        -- are NOT visible, and neither are the writes of a leader a newer term superseded.
}

---- Deferred Specifications

deferred ResolveTx                   -- tx_resolver.indexTx(msgId, timestamp, msg): resolve a source-log
                                     -- transaction (put/delete/erase logic), stage it on the resolver's
                                     -- queue and advance the RESOLVED head. Covers the LegacyTx payload
                                     -- overload too. See: live-index.allium CommitTransaction /
                                     -- AbortTransaction
deferred ResolveExternalSourceTx     -- tx_resolver.indexTx(msg, srcMsgId): assign the next tx_id off the
                                     -- RESOLVED head (head tx_id + 1), smooth system_time strictly
                                     -- greater than the previous tx's, run the caller's writer against a
                                     -- fresh OpenTx, and stage the result with the caller's durability
                                     -- handle. See: live-index.allium CommitTransaction
deferred ResolveDbOp
  -- Resolve an attach or detach as a ResolvedTx carrying the dbOp, for the catalog to be brought into
  -- line with when the record is read back.
deferred ResolveFailedDbOp
  -- Resolve an attach or detach that would be refused, as an ABORTED ResolvedTx carrying the caller
  -- fault that refused it.
deferred CheckCanAttach
  -- Whether an attach would be refused, and with which caller fault.
  -- Answered from the catalog's own records folded together with the dbOps the resolver still holds:
  -- the last one queued for a name says what will be there when this attach applies, and with none the
  -- catalog's own records stand.
  -- Reaches its verdict without changing anything.
deferred CheckCanDetach
  -- Whether a detach would be refused, answered the same way.
deferred ApplyDbOpToCatalog
  -- Bring the catalog into line with a committed dbOp, swallowing a caller fault: the transaction has
  -- already committed, so a node that cannot carry the instruction out holds none of a database the
  -- cluster agrees exists.
  -- See: database-lifecycle.allium AdmitInstructionCreatesEntry, RemoveBeginsTeardown
deferred TakeResolvedHead            -- tx_resolver.removeHead(): remove and return the OLDEST staged tx,
                                     -- passing ownership to the caller — which must then import it and
                                     -- close it, or fail its durability handle and free it, on every path.
                                     -- Removal precedes validation deliberately; see LeaderAppliesOwnTx
deferred ApplyResolvedTx             -- apply on consume-back: import the head's rows into the durable live
                                     -- tables, advance the DURABLE head to it, complete its durability
                                     -- handle, then free it. Frees it on ANY throw, so teardown's
                                     -- fail-pending can't double-close it.
                                     -- See: live-index.allium ApplyResolvedTx
deferred FailPendingHandles          -- resolver exit fails every still-staged tx's durability handle with
                                     -- the exit cause, before the channels close, so no executeTx caller
                                     -- waits forever. See: live-index.allium FailPendingHandles
deferred CloseResolver               -- phase 2 of the leader's two-phase close frees the resolver and the
                                     -- buffers of any un-applied staged tx; demotion is exactly this
                                     -- teardown, with no separate runtime drop.
                                     -- See: live-index.allium CloseResolver
